    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/cp-10-63-2014",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Millennial-scale variability associated with Dansgaard\u2013Oeschger events is arguably one of the most puzzling climate phenomena ever discovered in paleoclimate archives. Here, we set out to elucidate the underlying dynamics by conducting a transient global hindcast simulation with a 3-D intermediate complexity earth system model covering the period 50 to 30 ka BP. The model is forced by time-varying external boundary conditions (greenhouse gases, orbital forcing, and ice-sheet orography and albedo) and anomalous North Atlantic freshwater fluxes, which mimic the effects of changing northern hemispheric ice volume on millennial timescales. Together these forcings generate a realistic global climate trajectory, as demonstrated by an extensive model/paleo data comparison. Our results are consistent with the idea that variations in ice-sheet calving and subsequent changes of the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation were the main drivers for the continuum of glacial millennial-scale variability seen in paleorecords across the globe.",
    "field": "Paleoclimate dynamics",
    "problem": "The origin of prominent millennial-scale Dansgaard-Oeschger climate variability remains elusive, with numerous competing proposed mechanisms preventing a unified understanding of the dynamics that drove these massive and abrupt climate shifts during the last glacial period.",
    "method": "This paper hindcasts Dansgaard\u2013Oeschger variability using an earth system model (50-30 ka BP) forced by time-varying boundary conditions and anomalous North Atlantic freshwater fluxes, which are iteratively optimized to match subtropical sea surface temperature reconstructions.",
    "task": "The task is to use a global hindcast simulation with an earth system model to elucidate the mechanisms, patterns, and timing of Dansgaard\u2013Oeschger millennial-scale climate variability during the period 50 to 30 ka BP.",
