            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Moreover, possible feedbacks between the Atlantic meridional overturning circulation (AMOC) and ice sheets may have further modulated the evolution of iceberg and freshwater discharges into the North Atlantic as previously suggested (Schulz et al., 2002;",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Indonesian Throughflow",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Indonesian archipelago",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "According to Fig. 7h, changes in the barotropic transport across the Indonesian archipelago occur almost in unison with the AMOC. This surprisingly fast adjustment can be attained by two processes: (i) wind changes in the Pacific (Timmermann et al., 2005b), and (ii) fast oceanic adjustment processes involving wave propagation from the Atlantic into the Indian and Pacific oceans, as discussed in Timmermann et al. (2005a). The former can modulate the Indonesian Throughflow via the island rule (Godfrey, 1989), whereas the latter would have to change the joint effect of baroclinicity and relief (JEBAR) term in the barotropic transport equation (Sarkisyan and Ivanov, 1971; Cane et al., 1998).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
